      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "23e66809-3b46-59d9-bf6f-d09b334197dc",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: \"Just the Two of Us,\" \"Bette Davis Eyes,\" Winelight, \"In the Name of Love\"\n- People: Grover Washington Jr., Bill Withers, William Salter, Ralph MacDonald, Sheena Easton, Kim Carnes, Donna Weiss, Jackie DeShannon\n\nFacts:\n- In 1974, Jackie DeShannon recorded \"Bette Davis Eyes,\" a song written and composed by Donna Weiss and Jackie DeShannon.\n- In 1981, \"Just the Two of Us\" was released as an R&B single written by Bill Withers, William Salter, and Ralph MacDonald, and it was recorded by Grover Washington Jr. and Bill Withers.\n- In 1981, Kim Carnes's version of \"Bette Davis Eyes\" spent nine weeks at No. 1 on the Billboard Hot 100 and became Billboard's biggest hit of 1981.\n- An edited version of \"Just the Two of Us\" reached No. 2 on the Billboard Hot 100 and stayed there for three weeks behind Sheena Easton's \"Morning Train (9 to 5)\" and Kim Carnes's \"Bette Davis Eyes.\"\n- The Winelight track \"In the Name of Love\" was also released in a rearranged form without Grover Washington Jr.'s saxophone track under the name of Ralph MacDonald and Bill Withers, with Bill Withers on vocals.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d6b8a8fa-4484-5c4b-89d0-d647dbbf4a84",
        "text": "Page: Just the Two of Us (Grover Washington Jr. song). Sentence: \"Just the Two of Us\" is a 1981 R&B single written by Bill Withers, William Salter and Ralph MacDonald, which was recorded by Grover Washington Jr. and Bill Withers.\nPage: Just the Two of Us (Grover Washington Jr. song). Sentence: An edited version reached number two on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100, staying there for 3 weeks, behind \"Morning Train (9 to 5)\" by Sheena Easton and \"Bette Davis Eyes\" by Kim Carnes.\nPage: Bette Davis Eyes. Sentence: \"Bette Davis Eyes\" is a song written and composed by Donna Weiss and Jackie DeShannon, and made popular by American singer Kim Carnes.\nPage: Bette Davis Eyes. Sentence: DeShannon recorded it in 1974; Carnes's 1981 version spent nine weeks at No. 1 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100 and was \"Billboard\"' s biggest hit of 1981.\nPage: Winelight. Sentence: The track \"In the Name of Love\" from the album was also released in rearranged form, without Washington's saxophone track, under the name of Ralph MacDonald and Bill Withers (on vocals).",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
